.TH TIMED 8 "Peios" "timed 0.1.10" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
timed \- Peios authenticated network time client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/sbin/timed
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timed
keeps the system clock synchronized with configured network sources.  It is
the only service granted
.B SeSystemtimePrivilege
in the standard Peios image.  The daemon initiates NTP and Network Time
Security connections but listens on no network port.
.PP
At startup it raises a clock older than the recorded build epoch to that
lower bound. The epoch is stored in the source tree for reproducible builds.
This bootstrap aid does not establish correct time or guarantee certificate
validation.
.PP
Every time it steps the clock rather than adjusting its rate \(em at that
startup floor, on its own decision, or when asked to by
.BR "clock set" " \(em"
it writes a
.B timed.clock.stepped
event giving the clock's reading before and after, and the SID of whoever
acted: the caller for a requested set, timed's own service SID otherwise.  The event is described in
.BR /usr/share/evman/timed.evman .
.PP
Network Time Security is required by default.  Plain NTP is used only when an
administrator explicitly enables it for a source.  Source selection requires
agreement and rejects outliers rather than allowing a preferred source to
override the majority.
.PP
NTS authenticates the configured hostname, not a DNS canonical name.
The fallback sources are time.cloudflare.com, nts.netnod.se, and
ptbtime1.ptb.de. Roots are obtained for ServerAuth at startup and refreshed
after handshake failures; existing sessions and cached cookies are not
immediately invalidated by a trust-store change.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The daemon watches
.B Machine\\System\\Time
in the Peios registry.  Important values are:
.TP
.B Servers
A REG_MULTI_SZ list of hosts, optionally followed by
.B prefer
or
.BR unauthenticated .
.TP
.B AllowUnauthenticated
A REG_DWORD enabling explicitly marked plain-NTP sources.  The secure default
is zero.
.TP
.B UseFromDHCP
A REG_DWORD allowing DHCP option 42 when no explicit source list exists.  The
secure default is zero.
.TP
.BR MinPoll , " MaxPoll"
REG_DWORD base-two polling intervals, bounded by the daemon.
.PP
Complete field semantics and security notes are installed as the
.B timed.regman
registry manual.
.PP
A failed complete configuration read retains the previous running
configuration. An explicit reload reports this failure. Startup fails if
the initial configuration cannot be read.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /run/timed/time.sock
Local query and control socket.
.TP
.B /var/state/timed/drift
Persisted learned oscillator frequency.
.TP
.B /var/state/timed/cookies/
Persisted NTS cookies.  This directory is private to SYSTEM,
Administrators, and timed's service SID.
.TP
.B /var/state/timed/zone
The name of the zone timed last put in
.BR /etc/localtime ,
which carries no name of its own.  Believed at start only while
.B /etc/localtime
is still a copy of that zone's file, so that
.B clock status
names the zone in force even when
.B TimeZone
names one timed cannot use.
.TP
.B /etc/localtime
The machine's time zone, from
.BR "Machine\\System\\Time TimeZone" :
a copy timed makes of the zone's file under
.BR /usr/share/zoneinfo ,
again at every start.
.SH SERVICE INTEGRATION
The package supplies inert Registry Image Manager seeds for the service,
time policy, and the service-SID privilege grant.  An image must explicitly
select those seeds.  The service runs as LocalService; its pre-start hook runs
as SYSTEM only to provision the protected state directory, and
.B /etc/localtime
in the registry-rendered layer of
.BR /etc ,
which the service and authorized administrators can write.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clock (1),
.BR reg (1),
.BR svctl (1)

.SH STATE FILE VALIDATION
Installed zone files and zone metadata must be regular files no larger than
16 MiB. Symlinks to regular files are allowed for zone aliases. State reads
are bounded by their formats; malformed, oversized, or nonregular cache
files are ignored. Saved state uses exclusively created private staging
files, then rename, without reusing a pre-existing staging path.
.PP
The pre-created /etc/localtime file is updated in place because timed cannot
rename entries in its parent. Readers can observe an empty or partial file
during copying; a write failure can leave a partial copy.
